
<% content_for :title, "Loneliness & Isolation Relapse Plan | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="relapse-plan-page relapse-plan-loneliness">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-hero">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <%= link_to "← ALL RELAPSE PLANS",
          toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
          class: "relapse-plan-back" %>

      <p class="relapse-plan-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / RELAPSE PLANS / LONELINESS &amp; ISOLATION
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>JUST YOU</span>
        <span>AND YOUR</span>
        <span>LOUD BRAIN.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="relapse-plan-intro">
        YOUR SOBRIETY PLAN FOR FEELING ALONE
        &amp; PULLING AWAY.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / YOUR EXPERIENCE -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-yellow">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        01 / YOUR EXPERIENCE
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT ALONE<br>
        FEELS LIKE.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          Being alone isn't automatically a problem.
          Sometimes you genuinely enjoy your own company.
          You can work on a creative project, listen to music,
          spend time with Banjo, or enjoy an evening
          without having to answer to anyone.
        </p>

        <p>
          But there's a difference between choosing solitude
          and gradually disappearing from your own life.
          Sometimes being alone feels peaceful.
          Other times, the silence gets uncomfortable,
          your thoughts become louder, and you start
          wishing you could feel something different.
        </p>

        <p>
          Alcohol used to make being alone feel different.
          You didn't need a party or another person
          to have a reason to drink. Sometimes you
          drank alone because you were sad.
          Sometimes you drank because you were bored,
          wanted to relax, or simply enjoyed being drunk.
        </p>

        <p>
          Drinking could turn an ordinary evening
          into something you looked forward to.
          It offered pleasure, distraction, and
          temporary relief from whatever was
          happening inside your head.
        </p>

        <p>
          Loneliness can become more complicated
          during depression. You may withdraw
          because conversation feels exhausting,
          but spending more time alone can also
          leave you feeling increasingly disconnected.
          Physical pain and limited mobility
          can make that isolation harder to escape.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            You don't have to stop enjoying solitude.
            You need to recognize when being alone
            stops feeling like a choice and
            starts becoming a problem.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / YOUR WARNING SIGNS -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-black">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        02 / YOUR WARNING SIGNS
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHEN YOU START<br>
        DISAPPEARING.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- BEHAVIOR -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHAT YOU STOP DOING</h3>

          <p>
            You stop responding to messages,
            decline invitations, cancel plans,
            and gradually lose contact with
            people you normally enjoy.
          </p>

          <p>
            You may spend more time in bed,
            stop working on creative projects,
            lose interest in hobbies,
            or let entire days pass without
            much interaction.
          </p>

          <p>
            When depression is involved,
            even answering a simple text
            can feel like another responsibility.
            You tell yourself you'll respond
            tomorrow, and tomorrow turns
            into another week.
          </p>

          <p>
            Notice when solitude stops
            feeling restorative. If you're
            lonely, increasingly withdrawn,
            and no longer enjoying the time
            you spend alone, something
            may need attention.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- THOUGHTS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHAT YOU TELL YOURSELF</h3>

          <p>
            "Nobody wants to hear from me."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I don't have the energy
            to deal with people."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I'll reach out when
            I'm feeling better."
          </p>

          <p>
            "Everyone else has
            their own problems."
          </p>

          <p>
            "What's the point?"
          </p>

          <p>
            These thoughts can start
            sounding convincing when
            you're depressed or lonely.
            But feeling unwanted isn't
            evidence that you are unwanted.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for the point when
            your thinking shifts toward
            drinking: "At least I'd have
            something to do," or
            "I could just have a few
            drinks and enjoy myself."
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't need to argue
            with every negative thought.
            You can recognize that
            your interpretation may
            be affected by your mood
            and choose to make
            contact anyway.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / YOUR CONNECTION PLAN -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-pink">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        03 / YOUR CONNECTION PLAN
      </p>

      <h2>
        MAKE CONTACT<br>
        POSSIBLE.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- FIRST PERSON -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR FIRST PERSON</h3>

          <p>
            Start with someone you trust
            and feel comfortable contacting.
            You don't need the perfect
            person or a compelling reason
            to reach out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Send a text, make a call,
            or ask whether they're
            available to spend some
            time together.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be specific about what
            you're looking for.
            Maybe you want company,
            a distraction, someone
            to listen, or simply
            another person's presence.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can say you're having
            a difficult day without
            having to explain your
            entire mental health history.
          </p>

         

        </div>


        <!-- BACKUP CONNECTION -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR BACKUP CONNECTION</h3>

          <p>
            If your first person
            doesn't answer, don't
            interpret that as proof
            nobody wants to talk
            to you.
          </p>
.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can also arrange
            contact for another day.
            Having something planned
            gives you a connection
            to look forward to
            without requiring
            immediate social energy.
          </p>

          <p>
            One unanswered message
            doesn't have to determine
            how the rest of your
            evening goes.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- LOW-PRESSURE COMPANY -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>LOW-PRESSURE COMPANY</h3>

          <p>
            Connection doesn't
            have to mean a big
            social event or a
            serious conversation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Invite someone over
            to watch a movie.
            Work on a craft while
            someone keeps you company.
            Play an online game,
            talk on the phone,
            or exchange a few
            messages throughout
            the evening.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can also spend
            time with Banjo,
            listen to something
            familiar, or sit
            somewhere that helps
            you feel connected
            to your surroundings.
          </p>

          <p>
            If depression or pain
            limits your energy,
            choose an activity
            that doesn't require
            much preparation
            or physical effort.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't need to
            entertain someone
            to deserve their
            company.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- DRINKING ALONE -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHEN YOU WANT TO DRINK ALONE</h3>

          <p>
            If loneliness turns
            into wanting alcohol,
            recognize the connection.
            Are you looking for
            pleasure, relaxation,
            distraction, or a way
            to stop feeling alone?
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't pretend drinking
            alone was never enjoyable.
            Remember what you liked
            about it, then play
            the tape forward.
          </p>

          <p>
            Alcohol might make
            an empty evening
            feel different,
            but it won't
            provide the connection
            you're missing or
            resolve the problems
            underneath the urge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't buy alcohol
            or browse delivery
            options. Give yourself
            ten minutes to change
            what you're doing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Eat something,
            make a nonalcoholic
            drink, put on music,
            or contact someone
            who supports your
            sobriety.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the craving
            continues, tell
            someone directly.
            You don't need
            to handle a strong
            urge in isolation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              You can miss drinking
              alone without choosing
              to return to it.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>


      <!-- MESSAGE SCRIPT -->
      <div class="relapse-plan-script">

        <h3>A MESSAGE YOU CAN ACTUALLY SEND</h3>

        <p>
          "Hey, I've been isolating a bit
          and could use some company.
          Do you have time to talk
          or hang out sometime?
          Nothing serious.
          I just don't want to
          spend another evening
          completely in my own head."
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / IF YOU NEED MORE -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-purple">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        04 / IF YOU NEED MORE
      </p>

      <h2>
        YOUR NEXT<br>
        SUPPORT OPTION.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          If isolation is becoming
          a pattern rather than
          an occasional choice,
          take a closer look
          at what's happening.
          Are you withdrawing
          because you're depressed,
          physically limited,
          overwhelmed, or
          avoiding something?
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're losing interest
          in everyday life, spending
          increasing amounts of time
          alone, or struggling
          to maintain basic routines,
          contact your psych.
        </p>

        <p>
          Bipolar depression can
          last months, and isolation
          can make it harder
          to recognize how much
          your functioning has changed.
          Don't wait until you're
          completely disconnected
          before asking for help.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're seriously
          considering drinking,
          contact a trusted friend.
          Arrange company if possible,
          attend an online meeting,
          or follow your established
          relapse prevention plan.
        </p>

    

        <p>
          You don't need to become
          more outgoing or fill
          every hour with people.
          You can enjoy solitude,
          maintain a few meaningful
          relationships, and
          recognize when
          you need additional
          connection.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            Being alone isn't
            the problem.
            Losing connection
            with your life
            is what deserves
            attention.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>
<!-- BACK TO RELAPSE PLANS -->
<nav class="index-home-nav" aria-label="Return to relapse plans">
  <%= link_to "← BACK TO RELAPSE PLANS",
      toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
      class: "index-home-button" %>
</nav>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>
